ukraine's zaparisha nuclear power plant  which is held by russian forces remains  disconnected from the energy grid  causing blackouts across the region  the plant was knocked offline on  thursday due to shelling but has managed  to restore its own power  that means its cooling and safety  systems are still functional but  ukraine's president vladimir zolensky  says the world has narrowly avoided a  radiation disaster  before russia's invasion the vast  zaparisia nuclear plant supplied around  20 percent of ukraine's energy needs but  on thursday it was temporarily cut off  from the country's power grid entirely  after fires visible here on satellite  imagery knocked out its last external  power line  that forced the facility's last two  operating reactors to shut down  ukrainian officials say power has since  been restored  one reactor is back online and work is  reportedly underway to restore the other  russian forces have been in control of  the plant since march and ukraine has  blamed them for starting the fires but  russia claims they were sparked by  ukrainian shelling  for weeks now increased fighting of the  plant has raised fears of a nuclear  catastrophe  experts fear that a loss of power would  leave the facility forced to rely on  emergency backup generators to cool its  reactors  the diesel generators didn't turn on  if after the blackout the automatic  systems and the personnel didn't start  working  we would have to face the consequences  of a radiation accident  russia has put ukrainians as well as all  europeans one step away from radiation  disaster  catastrophe  the head of the un's nuclear watchdog  told french broadcaster france24 he's  planning to visit  i think now there is a general  recognition  that we need to be there we need to be  there soon  kiev  accepts it  moscow accepts it we need to go and we  are going to be there hopefully very  very soon  the un and world leaders have called for  the creation of a demilitarized zone  surrounding the plant to avert disaster  but russia has rejected that proposal  well for the latest i'm joined now by dw  correspondent roman gonciarenko in kiev  hello to you roman so tell us what is  the situation right now at the zappos  nuclear power plant  well the situation there remains very  volatile and it is not quite clear what  exactly is happening  um we had reports that the power station  was reconnected with the ukrainian power  grid after it was cut off for the first  time in its history  there were reports in ukrainian media  that one of the reactors was running  again then there was other reports that  both reactors are still shut down  and it is not quite clear if they will  um uh reconnect both of them during the  day probably yes uh this is what we  expect so uh this whole situation we had  some also a confirmation from satellite  images that there is a fire in a forest  nearby  which according to both sides the  russian and the ukrainian side caused  that cut off yesterday  so uh to clear the situation uh it shows  that the  delegation of the international atomic  agency  should go there it is being discussed it  is expected in the coming days but it's  still it is still not clear which route  they would take  through ukraine which is what kiev  insists on or through russia which kiev  says is not possible and um is against  it so we do not have that an answer to  that most important question which route  the the delegation of the international  experts would take to visit the station  but we can assume that that visit will  happen in the coming days  now do we have any information on  whether thursday's power cut in some way  affected the safety of the nuclear plant  in any way  well the ukrainian authorities are  saying that no  there was  everything was under control at that  time so we have ukrainian personnel on  that station you have to remember and  russian soldiers  there  and a lot of russian military equipment  according to the ukrainian source